1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,640
The cargo routing service passes every
unit test on a laptop and fails contract
tests in the pipeline with a bizarre bean:
FakeGpsClock from com.cargo.testsupport.

2
00:00:10,759 --> 00:00:14,879
Production logs show the clock advancing
in 15-minute jumps designed for demos.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,280
Someone set @ComponentScan("com.cargo") on
the Boot application in cargo-app,

4
00:00:20,399 --> 00:00:32,679
and the cargo-test-support JAR — depended
on as test scope in the library module but
somehow compile-scoped in the app — sat on
the runtime classpath with @Component
classes under com.cargo.testsupport.

5
00:00:32,799 --> 00:00:36,799
Scanning did exactly what it was told. The
package boundary did not.

6
00:00:36,919 --> 00:00:50,919
Component scanning walks packages looking
for stereotype annotations — @Component,
@Service, @Repository, @Controller, and
meta-annotated types — and registers bean
definitions for each hit. It is
convenience, not magic.

7
00:00:51,039 --> 00:01:03,319
Convenience without package discipline
becomes a production classpath mine:
anything annotated and reachable under the
scan base becomes a candidate singleton.
Look at this java for a moment. Walk that
fix.

8
00:01:03,439 --> 00:01:09,879
@SpringBootApplication already includes a
@ComponentScan defaulted to the main class
package.

9
00:01:09,999 --> 00:01:19,239
The explicit @ComponentScan replaces that
default with basePackages =
"com.cargo.routing" — only that tree and
its subpackages are candidates.

10
00:01:19,359 --> 00:01:31,959
excludeFilters with a REGEX pattern is
belt-and-suspenders if a testsupport class
somehow still shares the prefix; filter
types also include ANNOTATION,
ASSIGNABLE_TYPE, and ASPECTJ.

11
00:01:32,079 --> 00:01:43,759
Better still in many apps: put the
application class in com.cargo.routing and
rely on the default scan of that package
and below — never com.cargo if sibling
modules share the prefix.

12
00:01:43,879 --> 00:01:54,039
Keep test helpers in src/test or in a JAR
that production never depends on; scope
mistakes are half of this outage class.
Runtime of a scan during context refresh.

13
00:01:54,159 --> 00:02:01,559
ClassPathBeanDefinitionScanner (or Boot's
equivalent path) obtains candidate
resource paths under the base packages.

14
00:02:01,679 --> 00:02:16,359
For each .class resource it reads
*metadata* via a metadata reader — often
without fully initializing the class — and
checks for stereotype annotations and
include/exclude filters. Hits become
BeanDefinition entries.

15
00:02:16,479 --> 00:02:25,679
Default bean name is the short class name
decapitalized (fakeGpsClock), unless
@Component("explicit") overrides.

16
00:02:25,799 --> 00:02:32,719
Instantiation happens later when the bean
is needed or when non-lazy singletons are
pre-instantiated at end of refresh.

17
00:02:32,839 --> 00:02:41,279
That timing is why FakeGpsClock can become
a real singleton before any HTTP request:
the scan already decided it was a bean,

18
00:02:41,399 --> 00:02:51,359
and something depended on Clock or
GpsClock by type — or the fake was simply
created as an eager singleton and started
ticking in a @PostConstruct.

19
00:02:51,479 --> 00:03:03,039
Failure mode symptoms in the cargo outage:
contract tests assert arrival ETAs against
wall-clock windows and fail
intermittently; production metrics show
routes recalculated on a 15-minute grid;

20
00:03:03,159 --> 00:03:14,479
beans actuator (if exposed) lists
fakeGpsClock; debug logging of
ClassPathBeanDefinitionScanner shows
com.cargo.testsupport.FakeGpsClock as a
candidate.

21
00:03:14,599 --> 00:03:30,799
Another related symptom: duplicate type
matches — both SystemGpsClock and
FakeGpsClock implement GpsClock, startup
fails with
NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException, or
@Primary on the wrong one silently wins.
Trade-offs.

22
00:03:30,919 --> 00:03:41,599
Wide scans (com.company) onboard new
@Service classes with zero registration
code and maximize accidental inclusion of
demos, fakes, and abandoned packages.

23
00:03:41,719 --> 00:03:47,120
Narrow scans and explicit @Bean methods
cost a line of registration but make the
graph auditable.

24
00:03:47,240 --> 00:03:57,080
Filters help, but filters that exist
because the classpath is dirty are
treating the symptom; fixing Maven/Gradle
scopes and package layout treats the
cause.

25
00:03:57,200 --> 00:04:07,840
Scanning also interacts with multi-module
builds: a dependency's @Component classes
are visible if that dependency is on the
runtime classpath — "it's only a library"
is not isolation.

26
00:04:07,960 --> 00:04:19,800
Misconception unique to scanning:
"@SpringBootApplication only scans the
package of the main class, so deep package
trees are always safe." It scans that
package *and all subpackages*.

27
00:04:19,920 --> 00:04:30,800
If you place main in com.cargo to "see
everything," you opted into everything
under com.cargo, including modules you
forgot existed. FakeGpsClock is gone from
prod.

28
00:04:30,920 --> 00:04:41,480
The next fight is subtler: the same app
declares two DataSource beans — OLTP and
reporting — and developers disagree
whether @Autowired, @Resource,

29
00:04:41,600 --> 00:04:46,760
or @Inject will pick the right one when a
field is named reportingDataSource.
